      "latency_ms": 3105.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 760,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through scientific, physical, and ecological lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Definition\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. Its famous chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n\nThis means every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   Two hydrogen atoms\n*   One oxygen atom\n\nThese atoms are held together by strong covalent bonds. Furthermore, water molecules have a \"bent\" shape, which makes them **polar**. This means one side of the molecule (the oxygen side) has a slight negative charge, while the other side (the hydrogen side) has a slight positive charge. This polarity is what makes water such a great solvent—it can dissolve salt, sugar, and many other substances easily.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter within Earth's normal temperature range:\n\n*   **Liquid:** The standard, free-flowing water we drink, swim in, and use to wash.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water. Uniquely, solid water (ice) is **less dense** than liquid water, which is why ice floats. If ice sank, oceans and lakes would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air or steam rising from boiling water.\n\n**Other notable physical traits:**\n*   It has no color, taste, or smell in its pure form.\n*   It has a high **heat capacity**, meaning it can absorb or release a lot of heat without drastically changing its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and the internal temperature of living bodies.\n*   It exhibits **surface tension** (molecules stick tightly together), which is why water forms droplets and allows small insects to walk across its surface.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Global Scale (The Hydrosphere)\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth’s surface**. \n*   The vast majority of it (**96.5%**) is salt water found in the oceans.\n*   Only about **2.5%** is fresh water.\n*   Of that fresh water, most is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n\nWater is constantly moving around, above, and below the surface of the Earth through the **water cycle** (evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\nWater is the fundamental building block of life. \n*   **Human Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly 55% to 60% water. Our brains are about 73% water, and our lungs are about 83%. We need it to regulate body temperature, transport nutrients, flush out waste, and keep our cells functioning. You can survive weeks without food, but only a few days without water.\n*   **Ecosystems:** All plants and animals require water to survive. Plants use water, sunlight, and carbon dioxide to make their own food through photosynthesis.\n\nIn short, water is the lifeblood of our planet—a simple molecule with remarkably complex and life-sustaining properties."
